<\body>
  <doc-data|<doc-title|A Constructive Introduction to Quantum Field
  Theory>|<doc-subtitle|Lecture 5: <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> on the
  torus I. The Wetterich FBSDE, two renormalisations and the large-field
  problem>|<doc-author|<author-data|<author-name|M.
  Gubinelli>|<author-affiliation|Mathematical Institute, University of
  Oxford>>>|<doc-date|November 2026 <emdash> <em|provisional>>>

  <abstract-data|<\abstract>
    The last three lectures construct the massive
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> measure on the three-dimensional torus
    with the forward\Ubackward equations of Lecture<nbsp>4. In this lecture
    we set up the problem. For the mass regulator the Polchinski process of
    Lecture<nbsp>4 is the linear estimator <math|C<rsub|q>*P<rsub|q>> built
    from noisy observations of the field, and its forecast is their posterior
    mean; this gives an exact FBSDE at every lattice cutoff. Its backward
    half is governed by the Wetterich equation. We cannot solve that
    equation, so we construct an explicit approximate effective average
    action and write the exact FBSDE in the coordinates it defines: the
    <em|approximate forecast> <math|Y> and the <em|error> <math|Z>. The error
    is driven only by the failure of the approximate action to solve the
    Wetterich equation. We explain why the approximate action needs two
    renormalisations, in two senses: a linearly and a logarithmically
    divergent mass counterterm, and two correction steps beyond one loop,
    which make the defect integrable in the backward equation. Finally we
    identify the large-field problem: the construction only controls fields
    of size <math|r<rsub|q>=<around|(|q/\<gamma\>|)><rsup|1/2>>, and must be
    extended to all fields without losing coercivity. The price is an error
    that is not small but lives on large fields. Lecture<nbsp>6 controls it
    by a priori estimates on the FBSDE, and Lecture<nbsp>7 removes the
    cutoffs.
  </abstract>>

