  <section*|Introduction>

  Lecture<nbsp>4 formulated a programme (Figure<nbsp><hlink|7.1|cqft_lecture4.tm#fig:fbsde-flow>
  and the remark following it): solve the FBSDE

  <\equation*>
    \<Phi\><rsub|t>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*F<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|s>,<space|2em>F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around*|[|-D*V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>,
  </equation*>

  with a renormalised interaction <math|V<rsub|T>>, with estimates uniform in
  the ultraviolet cutoff, and take the limit. For
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> the force <math|F<rsub|t>> is not expected
  to converge to an integrable terminal value, and the naive estimates of the
  force diverge (Section<nbsp><hlink|10.2|cqft_lecture4.tm#sec:pert>). In
  these three lectures we carry out a version of the programme on the unit
  torus <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|3>>, for a large mass. The guiding principle is
  the one of Section<nbsp><hlink|8|cqft_lecture4.tm#sec:wetterich>: do not
  estimate <math|\<Phi\>> and <math|F> directly, but pass to the forecast
  coordinate, in which the forward equation has no drift and the tree
  diagrams have been integrated exactly.

  The argument has three parts, one per lecture.

  <\enumerate-roman>
    <item><em|Deterministic renormalisation> (this lecture). Construct an
    explicit approximate effective average action
    <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> and a counterterm, by a finite
    number of steps of the Wetterich flow, and estimate its defect.

    <item><em|A priori estimates> (Lecture<nbsp>6). Write the exact FBSDE in
    the coordinates of <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>>, and estimate
    the forward and the backward equation together, uniformly in the lattice.

    <item><em|Removing the cutoffs> (Lecture<nbsp>7). Remove the upper scale
    at fixed lattice, then the lattice, and identify the limiting FBSDE and
    its terminal law.
  </enumerate-roman>

  The emphasis is on the structure of the argument. We give complete proofs
  of the identities and of the scalar models of the estimates, and only
  describe the tensor estimates on the lattice. The lectures follow the
  course notes<nbsp><cite|FBSDE26>, where all the estimates are proved. The
  point of view is that of<nbsp><cite|BCG26>; for other constructions of
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> see<nbsp><cite|GJ73|FO76|BFS83|BG20|GH21>.

  <paragraph|What is proved.> The construction is for a large mass
  <math|m<rsup|2>=q<rsub|0>> compared to the coupling,
  <math|\<lambda\>\<ll\>q<rsub|0><rsup|1/2>>: a weak dimensionless coupling
  on a fixed torus. It gives a subsequential limit of the lattice measures,
  with a limiting FBSDE driven by a Brownian motion obtained as the limit of
  the lattice innovations. It does not prove uniqueness of the limit, the
  Osterwalder\USchrader axioms, or an infinite-volume limit.

  <assign|section-nr|11><section|The problem and the exact
  FBSDE><label|sec:problem>

  <subsection|Lattice, scales and notation><label|sec:notation>

  Let <math|N> be an odd integer and <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|N>=<around|(|N<rsup|-1>*\<bbb-Z\>/\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|3>>
  the periodic lattice of mesh <math|N<rsup|-1>> in the unit torus. Let
  <math|H<rsub|N>=\<bbb-R\><rsup|\<Lambda\><rsub|N>>> with

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>=<big|int>u*v,<space|2em><big|int>f\<assign\>N<rsup|-3>*<big|sum><rsub|x\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|N>>f<around|(|x|)>,
  </equation*>

  so that the volume is <math|1> and integrals approximate continuum
  integrals. The nearest-neighbour Laplacian is
  <math|\<Delta\><rsub|N>*u<around|(|x|)>=N<rsup|2>*<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|3><around|[|u*<around|(|x+e<rsub|i>/N|)>+u*<around|(|x-e<rsub|i>/N|)>-2*u<around|(|x|)>|]>>;
  we usually drop the index <math|N>. A kernel <math|C*<around|(|x,z|)>> acts
  by <math|C*u<around|(|x|)>=<big|int>C*<around|(|x,z|)>*u<around|(|z|)>\<mathd\>z>,
  so the identity has kernel <math|N<rsup|3>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|x=z>>,
  and

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Tr>C=<big|int>C*<around|(|x,x|)>\<mathd\>x,<space|2em><around|(|<math-up|diag>C|)><around|(|x|)>=C*<around|(|x,x|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|C|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|row>>=sup<rsub|x>
    <big|int><around|\||C*<around|(|x,z|)>|\|>\<mathd\>z.
  </equation*>

  <math|M<rsub|f>> is multiplication by <math|f>, and
  <math|K+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>> means <math|K+\<gamma\>*M<rsub|y<rsup|2>>>.
  For a functional <math|F> on <math|H<rsub|N>>, <math|D*F>,
  <math|D<rsup|2>*F>, <math|D<rsup|k>*F> are its gradient and derivatives, as
  in Lecture<nbsp>3. <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>> is the
  <math|L<rsup|p>> norm for the measure <math|<big|int>>.

  <paragraph|The resolution parameter.> Lecture<nbsp>3 used a general
  covariance profile. Here we use the <em|mass-regulator profile> of
  Exercise<nbsp><hlink|8.9|cqft_lecture4.tm#ex:regulators> of Lecture 4: with
  <math|m<rsup|2>=q<rsub|0>> and <math|t\<ge\>0>,

  <\equation*>
    <wide|Q|^><rsub|t><around|(|k|)>=<frac|1|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+q<rsub|0>>-<frac|1|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+q<rsub|0>+t<rsup|2>>,<space|2em><with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|t>=t<rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  It is convenient to parametrise the resolution by the squared regulator
  mass

  <\equation*>
    q\<assign\>q<rsub|0>+t<rsup|2>\<in\><around|[|q<rsub|0>,\<infty\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:K-C>K<rsub|q>\<assign\>q-\<Delta\><rsub|N>,<space|2em>C<rsub|q>\<assign\>K<rsub|q><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>C\<assign\>C<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\><rsub|N>|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation>

  In the notation of Lecture<nbsp>3, with <math|T=\<infty\>> and using
  <math|q> instead of <math|t> as the resolution variable (so
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>>> now means <math|\<partial\><rsub|q>*Q<rsub|q>>),

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:dictionary-profile>Q<rsub|q>=C-C<rsub|q>,<space|2em>\<partial\><rsub|q>*Q<rsub|q>=C<rsub|q><rsup|2>,<space|2em><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsup|1/2>=C<rsub|q>,<space|2em>S<rsub|q>=C<rsub|q>,<space|2em><with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|q>=q-q<rsub|0>,<space|2em>\<partial\><rsub|q>*<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|q>=1.
  </equation>

  So <math|C<rsub|q>> is the covariance of the scales not yet resolved, and
  <math|K<rsub|q>=S<rsub|q><rsup|-1>> is the precision of the posterior at
  resolution <math|q> under the free prior <math|\<mu\><rsup|C>>. The main
  properties of <math|C<rsub|q>> are elementary.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Free resolvents><label|lem:free> For <math|q\<geq\>1>, uniformly
    in <math|N>: <math|C<rsub|q>> has a nonnegative kernel,
    <math|C<rsub|q>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>=q<rsup|-1>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>>,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\>p>=q<rsup|-1>> for
    <math|1\<le\>p\<le\>\<infty\>>, and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:a2>a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>\<assign\><around|(|C<rsub|q><rsup|2>|)><around|(|x,x|)>=<math-up|Tr>C<rsub|q><rsup|2>\<lesssim\>q<rsup|-1/2>\<wedge\>N<rsup|3>*q<rsup|-2>,<space|2em><math-up|Tr>C<rsub|q>\<lesssim\>N\<wedge\>N<rsup|3>*q<rsup|-1>.
    </equation>

    For <math|y\<in\>H<rsub|N>> and <math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0>, the killed
    resolvent <math|G<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<around|(|K<rsub|q>+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>
    satisfies <math|0\<le\>G<rsub|q><around|(|y|)><around|(|x,z|)>\<le\>C<rsub|q>*<around|(|x,z|)>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <math|C<rsub|q>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-q*s>\<mathe\><rsup|s*\<Delta\>>\<mathd\>s>
    and <math|\<mathe\><rsup|s*\<Delta\>>> is the transition kernel of the
    lattice random walk. This gives positivity and the row sums. Killing the
    walk at rate <math|\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>> gives the comparison for
    <math|G<rsub|q>>. The symbol of <math|-\<Delta\><rsub|N>> is comparable
    to <math|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>> on the centred frequency cube
    <math|n\<in\><around|{|-<frac|N-1|2>,\<ldots\>,<frac|N-1|2>|}><rsup|3>>
    (symbol <math|4*N<rsup|2>*<big|sum><rsub|i>sin<rsup|2><around|(|\<pi\>*n<rsub|i>/N|)>>,
    with <math|k=2*\<pi\>*n>), and <math|<big|sum><rsub|k><around|(|q+<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-2>\<le\>C*<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>><around|(|q+<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-2>\<mathd\>k=C*q<rsup|-1/2>>.
    Counting the <math|N<rsup|3>> modes gives the other bounds.
  </proof>

  The <em|bubble> <math|a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>> is the coincident-point
  variance of the shell at resolution <math|q>:
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|q><math-up|Var><around|(|X<rsub|q><around|(|x|)>|)>=a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>>
  for the free field at resolution <math|q>, and
  <math|<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|\<infty\>>a<rsub|2>=<math-up|Tr>C<rsub|q<rsub|0>>\<asymp\>N>
  is the divergent variance of the lattice field.

  <subsection|The cutoff measure and the goal>

  Fix <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0> and put <math|\<gamma\>\<assign\>3*\<lambda\>>
  (not to be confused with the counterterm <math|\<gamma\><rsub|T>> of
  Lecture<nbsp>4). The interaction and the lattice measure are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:nuN>U<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<frac|\<lambda\>|4>*<big|int>\<varphi\><rsup|4>,<space|2em>V<rsub|N><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=U<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>+<frac|\<mu\><rsub|N>|2>*<big|int>\<varphi\><rsup|2>,<space|2em>\<nu\><rsub|N><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>=<frac|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|N><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>|\<cal-Z\><rsub|N>>*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsup|C><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|\<mu\><rsup|C>> is the centred Gaussian measure with covariance
  <math|C=C<rsub|q<rsub|0>>> on <math|H<rsub|N>>, and
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> is a mass counterterm to be chosen.
  With this normalisation <math|D<rsup|2>*U<around|(|y|)>=\<gamma\>*M<rsub|y<rsup|2>>>.
  In Lecture<nbsp>4 we wrote the interaction as
  <math|\<lambda\>*<big|int>H<rsub|4>*<around|(|\<varphi\>;c<rsub|T>|)>+\<gamma\><rsub|T>*<big|int>H<rsub|2>*<around|(|\<varphi\>;c<rsub|T>|)>>;
  the Wick ordering is now part of <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>>, and the coupling is
  rescaled by <math|4>. For every real <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>>,
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>> is a probability measure with all moments, since the
  quartic term dominates.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Main result, informal<nbsp><cite|FBSDE26>><label|thm:main> Let
    <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0> and let <math|q<rsub|0>> be large enough,
    depending on <math|\<lambda\>>. There are explicit <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>>,
    with <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>=-3*\<lambda\><math-up|Tr>C+O<around|(|log
    N|)>>, such that:

    <\enumerate-alpha>
      <item>the laws <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>>, embedded in
      <math|H<rsup|-s><around|(|\<bbb-T\><rsup|3>|)>>, are tight for every
      <math|s\<gtr\><frac|1|2>>;

      <item>along a subsequence, the forecast processes converge, on a common
      probability space, to a solution of a forward\Ubackward system on
      <math|<around|[|q<rsub|0>,\<infty\>|)>> driven by a cylindrical
      Brownian motion, whose terminal value has the limiting law
      <math|\<nu\>>;

      <item><math|\<nu\>> satisfies the integration by parts formula of
      Theorem<nbsp><hlink|20.5|cqft_lecture7.tm#thm:DS>, in which the
      renormalised cube appears only tested in expectation.
    </enumerate-alpha>
  </theorem>

  The bounds that prove (a) and (b) are uniform in <math|N>: this is <em|UV
  stability>. They are bounds on the <em|error> <math|Z> of an approximate
  forecast, defined below. We now set up the exact FBSDE at fixed <math|N>.

  <subsection|The Polchinski process as a posterior><label|sec:posterior>

  At a fixed lattice everything can be realised by a simple statistical
  experiment. Let <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>\<sim\>\<nu\><rsub|N>>, and
  let <math|B> be an independent Brownian motion on <math|H<rsub|N>>,
  <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|\<langle\>|B<rsub|t>,u|\<rangle\>><around|\<langle\>|B<rsub|s>,v|\<rangle\>>=<around|(|t\<wedge\>s|)><around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>>.
  We observe

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:observation>P<rsub|q>=<around|(|q-q<rsub|0>|)>*\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>+B<rsub|q-q<rsub|0>>,<space|2em>q\<ge\>q<rsub|0>,
  </equation>

  with the filtration <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|q>> generated by
  <math|<around|(|P<rsub|s>|)><rsub|s\<le\>q>> (completed), and
  <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>=\<bbb-E\>[<space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em><around|\||\<cal-F\><rsub|q>|]>>.
  Let

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:K-ex>\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|p|)>\<assign\>log
    <big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|<around|\<langle\>|p,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>-<frac|q-q<rsub|0>|2><around|\<\|\|\>|\<varphi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>>\<nu\><rsub|N><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>,<space|2em>\<nu\><rsub|q,p><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>\<assign\>\<mathe\><rsup|<around|\<langle\>|p,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>-<frac|q-q<rsub|0>|2>\<\|\|\>\<varphi\>\<\|\|\><rsub|2><rsup|2>-\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|p|)>>\<nu\><rsub|N><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>.
  </equation>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Posterior, forecast and innovation><label|prop:posterior> At every
    fixed <math|N>:

    <\enumerate-alpha>
      <item>the conditional law of <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>> given
      <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|q>> is <math|\<nu\><rsub|q,P<rsub|q>>>. In
      particular the <em|forecast> and the <em|conditional covariance> are

      <\equation>
        <label|eq:forecast>\<psi\><rsub|q>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>=D*\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|P<rsub|q>|)>,<space|2em><math-up|Cov><rsub|q>\<assign\><math-up|Cov><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>\|\<cal-F\><rsub|q>|)>=D<rsup|2>*\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|P<rsub|q>|)>\<gtr\>0;
      </equation>

      <item>the <em|innovation> <math|W<rsub|q>\<assign\>P<rsub|q>-<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q>\<psi\><rsub|s>\<mathd\>s>
      is an <math|<around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|q>|)>>-Brownian motion on
      <math|H<rsub|N>>, and

      <\equation>
        <label|eq:dpsi>\<mathd\>P<rsub|q>=\<psi\><rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q+\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>,<space|2em>\<mathd\>\<psi\><rsub|q>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>;
      </equation>

      <item><math|\<cal-K\>> solves <math|\<partial\><rsub|q>*\<cal-K\><rsub|q>+<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr>D<rsup|2>*\<cal-K\><rsub|q>+<frac|1|2>*<around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<cal-K\><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>=0>.
    </enumerate-alpha>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    (a) Conditionally on <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>=\<varphi\>>, the
    density of the observed path on <math|<around|[|q<rsub|0>,q|]>> with
    respect to the law of <math|B> is <math|exp
    <around|{|<around|\<langle\>|P<rsub|q>,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>-<frac|q-q<rsub|0>|2><around|\<\|\|\>|\<varphi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|}>>
    (Girsanov, or a direct computation on finitely many increments). Bayes'
    formula gives <math|\<nu\><rsub|q,P<rsub|q>>>, and differentiating
    <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|q>> in <math|p> gives the conditional moments.
    Positivity of <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|q>> holds because
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|q,p>> has a positive density. (b) For
    <math|s\<less\>q>, <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|s>*<around|[|P<rsub|q>-P<rsub|s>|]>=<around|(|q-s|)>*\<psi\><rsub|s>>
    since <math|B> has independent increments, and
    <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|s>*<big|int><rsub|s><rsup|q>\<psi\><rsub|u>\<mathd\>u=<around|(|q-s|)>*\<psi\><rsub|s>>
    since <math|\<psi\>> is a martingale. So <math|W> is a continuous
    martingale with <math|\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|W,W|\<rangle\>><rsub|q>=I<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q>,
    and Lévy's theorem applies. (c) <math|\<partial\><rsub|q>*\<cal-K\>=-<frac|1|2>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\><rsub|q,p>><around|\<\|\|\>|\<varphi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
    and <math|<math-up|Tr>D<rsup|2>*\<cal-K\>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\><rsub|q,p>>\<\|\|\>\<varphi\>\<\|\|\><rsup|2>-<around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<cal-K\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.
    Differentiating (c) in <math|p> and applying Itô's formula along
    <math|\<mathd\>P=D*\<cal-K\><around|(|P|)>\<mathd\>q+\<mathd\>W>, the
    drift of <math|D*\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|P<rsub|q>|)>> cancels, which
    gives <math|\<mathd\>\<psi\>=<math-up|Cov><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W>.
  </proof>

  The observation <math|P<rsub|q>> is not yet the field of Lecture<nbsp>4.
  The posterior <eqref|eq:K-ex> is <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>> tilted by a Gaussian
  factor of precision <math|q-q<rsub|0>>: the remaining randomness has
  precision <math|K<rsub|q<rsub|0>>+<around|(|q-q<rsub|0>|)>=K<rsub|q>>. This
  suggests the following.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The Polchinski process><label|prop:polchinski> Let
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>\<assign\>C<rsub|q>*P<rsub|q>> and
    <math|F<rsub|q>\<assign\>K<rsub|q>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>-\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)>>.
    Then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:polchinski-fbsde>\<mathd\>\<Phi\><rsub|q>=C<rsub|q><rsup|2>*F<rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q+C<rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>,<space|2em>F<rsub|q>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*<around*|[|-D*V<rsub|N><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]>,<space|2em>\<psi\><rsub|q>=\<Phi\><rsub|q>+C<rsub|q>*F<rsub|q>.
    </equation>

    This is the FBSDE <hlink|(7.7)|cqft_lecture4.tm#eq:fbsde> of
    Lecture<nbsp>4 for the profile <eqref|eq:dictionary-profile>:
    <math|\<Phi\>> is the interacting field at resolution <math|q>, <math|F>
    the effective force, <math|\<psi\>> the forecast of
    Proposition<nbsp><hlink|8.1|cqft_lecture4.tm#prop:psi>, and
    <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|q>=H<rsup|<math-up|ex>><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>>
    (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:wetterich>) is the response of
    Proposition<nbsp><hlink|8.3|cqft_lecture4.tm#prop:H-cov>. Under
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>=\<mu\><rsup|C>> one has <math|F=0>,
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>=\<psi\><rsub|q>>, and <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>> is the
    Gaussian field <math|X<rsub|q>> of Proposition<nbsp><hlink|5.2|cqft_lecture3.tm#prop:scale-GFF>
    of Lecture 3, with law <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|q>>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <math|\<mathd\>\<Phi\><rsub|q>=-C<rsub|q><rsup|2>*P<rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q+C<rsub|q>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>\<mathd\>q+\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>|)>=C<rsub|q>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>-\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)>\<mathd\>q+C<rsub|q>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>>,
    which is the first identity. For the second, integrate by parts in the
    density of <math|\<nu\><rsub|q,p>> with respect to Lebesgue measure,
    <math|exp <around|{|<around|\<langle\>|p,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,K<rsub|q>*\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>-V<rsub|N><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>|}>>:
    there is no boundary term, and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:posterior-ibp>p=K<rsub|q>*<space|0.17em>D*\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|p|)>+<big|int>D*V<rsub|N><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<nu\><rsub|q,p>.
    </equation>

    At <math|p=P<rsub|q>=K<rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|q>> this reads
    <math|K<rsub|q>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>-\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*<around|[|-D*V<rsub|N><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]>>.
    In the Gaussian case the posterior is
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|C<rsub|q>>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>-C<rsub|q>*P<rsub|q>|)>>,
    so <math|\<psi\>=\<Phi\>> and <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>-\<Phi\><rsub|q>>
    is independent of <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|q>> with covariance
    <math|C<rsub|q>>. Hence <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>> is a Gaussian martingale
    with covariance <math|C-C<rsub|q>=Q<rsub|q>>. In general both the law of
    <math|<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>,<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)><rsub|q>|)>>
    here and the law of <math|<around|(|X<rsub|\<infty\>>,<around|(|X<rsub|q>|)><rsub|q>|)>>
    under the measure <math|\<bbb-P\>> of
    Proposition<nbsp><hlink|7.2|cqft_lecture4.tm#prop:marginals> are the
    Gaussian law tilted by the same function
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|N>>/\<cal-Z\><rsub|N>> of the terminal
    field, so they coincide. Since <math|P<rsub|q>=K<rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|q>>,
    the filtration <math|<around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|q>|)>> is also the one
    generated by <math|\<Phi\>>.
  </proof>

  So at a fixed lattice the FBSDE of Lecture<nbsp>4 is a <em|theorem>, with a
  Brownian motion <math|W> which is explicitly the innovation of a filtering
  problem. All objects of Section<nbsp><hlink|8|cqft_lecture4.tm#sec:wetterich>
  have a statistical meaning:

  <\padded-center>
    <with|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|7|7|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Lecture
    4>|<cell|filtering at resolution <math|q>>>|<row|<cell|field
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>>>|<cell|<math|C<rsub|q>*P<rsub|q>>: posterior mean
    under the Gaussian prior <math|\<mu\><rsup|C>>>>|<row|<cell|forecast
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|q>>>|<cell|posterior mean
    <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>>>>|<row|<cell|response
    <math|H<rsup|<math-up|ex>><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|q>>>|<cell|posterior
    covariance>>|<row|<cell|force <math|F<rsub|q>=K<rsub|q>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>-\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)>>>|<cell|posterior
    mean of the terminal force <math|-D*V<rsub|N><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>>>>|<row|<cell|Brownian
    motion <math|W>>|<cell|innovation <math|P<rsub|q>-<big|int>\<psi\>>>>|<row|<cell|unresolved
    covariance <math|S<rsub|q>=C<rsub|q>>>|<cell|prior precision plus
    observation precision, inverted>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  The observations need no ultraviolet regularity: <math|P<rsub|q>> is
  defined for every <math|q>, with terminal resolution <math|\<infty\>> at
  fixed <math|N>. The lattice is the only ultraviolet cutoff, and
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>\<to\>\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>> as
  <math|q\<to\>\<infty\>> (Lemma<nbsp><hlink|18.1|cqft_lecture7.tm#lem:terminal>).

  <subsection|The Wetterich formulation><label|sec:wetterich>

  Let <math|\<Psi\><rsub|q>\<assign\>\<cal-K\><rsub|q><rsup|\<ast\>>> be the
  Legendre transform of the log-partition function and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:Gamma-ex>\<Gamma\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>\<assign\>\<Psi\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>-<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|y,K<rsub|q>*y|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>H<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ex>><around|(|y|)>\<assign\><around*|(|K<rsub|q>+D<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation>

  Comparing with <hlink|(8.11)|cqft_lecture4.tm#eq:moreau>, and using
  <math|S<rsub|q><rsup|-1>=K<rsub|q>>, <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|q>> is, up to a
  function of <math|q> alone, the <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> of Lecture<nbsp>4:
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|q>+<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|y,K<rsub|q<rsub|0>>*y|\<rangle\>>>
  is the effective average action with the regulator
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|R><rsub|q>=q-q<rsub|0>>
  (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:same-Gamma>). The coordinate identity
  <math|P<rsub|q>=D*\<Psi\><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>> reads

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coordinate-ex>K<rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|q>=K<rsub|q>*\<psi\><rsub|q>+D*\<Gamma\><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>,<space|2em><text|i.e.><space|2em>\<Phi\><rsub|q>=\<psi\><rsub|q>+C<rsub|q>*D*\<Gamma\><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>,
  </equation>

  which is <hlink|(8.5)|cqft_lecture4.tm#eq:coord> with
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|q>=D*\<Gamma\><rsub|q>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Wetterich equation with a mass regulator><label|prop:wetterich> At
    fixed <math|N>, <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|q>> is smooth and strictly convex
    after adding <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|y,K<rsub|q>*y|\<rangle\>>>,
    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:wetterich>\<partial\><rsub|q>*\<Gamma\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr>H<rsup|<math-up|ex>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>,<space|2em><math-up|Cov><rsub|q>=H<rsup|<math-up|ex>><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>.
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|q>> is smooth, strictly convex (its Hessian is a
    covariance) and superlinear (the posterior has full support), so
    <math|\<Psi\><rsub|q>> is smooth and <math|D<rsup|2>*\<Psi\><rsub|q>*<around|(|D*\<cal-K\><around|(|p|)>|)>=<around|(|D<rsup|2>*\<cal-K\><around|(|p|)>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    By the envelope theorem at fixed <math|y> and
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:posterior>(c),
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|q>*\<Psi\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=-\<partial\><rsub|q>*\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|p|)>=<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr>D<rsup|2>*\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|p|)>+<frac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>,
    with <math|p=D*\<Psi\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>>. Subtract
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|q><frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|y,K<rsub|q>*y|\<rangle\>>=<frac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.
  </proof>

  This is <hlink|(8.12)|cqft_lecture4.tm#eq:wetterich-Gamma> with
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|q><with|math-font|cal*|R>=1>, up to the constant
  <math|-<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr>C<rsub|q>> (which is infinite in the continuum
  and irrelevant here). It is an identity about the unknown measure
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>>, not a construction: to use it we would need
  estimates on <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|q>>, which is what we want to prove.
  Lecture<nbsp>4 suggests the strategy: since the FBSDE in forecast
  coordinates is

  <\equation*>
    \<mathd\>\<psi\><rsub|q>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>,<space|2em><math-up|Cov><rsub|q>=H<rsup|<math-up|ex>><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>,
  </equation*>

  an <em|approximate> effective action gives approximate forecast
  coordinates, and the error should be governed by how badly the approximate
  action fails to solve <eqref|eq:wetterich>.

  <subsection|Approximate forecast and error><label|sec:YZ>

  Let <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> be an even functional on
  <math|H<rsub|N>>, smooth in <math|q> and <math|y>, such that
  <math|<wide|\<Psi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|y,K<rsub|q>*y|\<rangle\>>+<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>>
  is uniformly convex, <math|D<rsup|2>*<wide|\<Psi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>\<ge\><wide*|c|\<bar\>><rsub|q>I>
  with <math|<wide*|c|\<bar\>><rsub|q>\<gtr\>0>. Let
  <math|<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>=<wide|\<Psi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><rsup|\<ast\>>>
  and define the <em|approximate response>, the <em|defect> and its drift

  <eqnarray*|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>\<assign\><around*|(|K<rsub|q>+D<rsup|2>*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|)><rsup|-1>,>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|e<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>\<assign\>\<partial\><rsub|q>*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>-<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around*|[|H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>-H<rsub|q><around|(|0|)>|]>,<space|2em>b<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>\<assign\>-H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>*D*e<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>.>|<eq-number><label|eq:defect>>>>>

  The subtraction of <math|H<rsub|q><around|(|0|)>> only removes a function
  of <math|q>. Define the <em|approximate forecast> and the <em|error>

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:YZ>Y<rsub|q>\<assign\>D*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|P<rsub|q>|)>,<space|2em>Z<rsub|q>\<assign\>\<psi\><rsub|q>-Y<rsub|q>,<space|2em>\<Xi\><rsub|q>\<assign\><math-up|Cov><rsub|q>-H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>.
  </equation>

  Equivalently <math|Y<rsub|q>> solves the approximate coordinate equation
  <math|K<rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|q>=K<rsub|q>*Y<rsub|q>+D*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>>,
  i.e. <eqref|eq:coordinate-ex> with <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>> in place
  of <math|\<Gamma\>>. If <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>=\<Gamma\>> then
  <math|Y=\<psi\>>, <math|Z=0>, and <math|e> depends only on <math|q>, so
  <math|b=0>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|The FBSDE in approximate forecast coordinates><label|thm:YZ> At
    every fixed <math|N>,

    <align|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mathd\>Y<rsub|q>>|<cell|=<around*|[|b<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>*Z<rsub|q>|]>\<mathd\>q+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>,>>|<row|<cell|\<mathd\>Z<rsub|q>>|<cell|=-<around*|[|b<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>*Z<rsub|q>|]>\<mathd\>q+\<Xi\><rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>,<eq-number><label|eq:YZ-fbsde>>>>>>

    with <math|Y<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=Z<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0>, and <math|Y+Z=\<psi\>>
    is the martingale <math|<big|int><math-up|Cov><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    By the Legendre identities <math|\<partial\><rsub|q>*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|p|)>=-\<partial\><rsub|q>*<wide|\<Psi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=-<frac|1|2>\<\|\|\>y\<\|\|\><rsup|2>-\<partial\><rsub|q>*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>>
    and <math|D<rsup|2>*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|p|)>=H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>>
    at <math|y=D*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|p|)>>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|q>*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>+<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr>D<rsup|2>*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>+<tfrac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|D*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=-<around*|(|\<partial\><rsub|q>*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>-<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr>H<rsub|q>|)>\<circ\>D*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>=-e<rsub|q>\<circ\>D*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>+<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr>H<rsub|q><around|(|0|)>.
    </equation*>

    Differentiate in <math|p>: <math|\<partial\><rsub|q>*D*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>>+<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr>D<rsup|2>*<around|(|D*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>>|)>+D<rsup|2>*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>>*<space|0.17em>D*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>>=-H*<space|0.17em>D*e=b>.
    Now apply Itô's formula to <math|Y<rsub|q>=D*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|P<rsub|q>|)>>
    along <math|\<mathd\>P=\<psi\><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q+\<mathd\>W>:

    <\equation*>
      \<mathd\>Y<rsub|q>=<around*|[|\<partial\><rsub|q>*D*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>>+<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr>D<rsup|2>*<around|(|D*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>>|)>|]>\<mathd\>q+D<rsup|2>*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>>*<space|0.17em><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>\<mathd\>q+\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>|)>=<around*|[|b<rsub|q>-H<rsub|q>*Y<rsub|q>+H<rsub|q>*\<psi\><rsub|q>|]>\<mathd\>q+H<rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>,
    </equation*>

    which is the first equation, since <math|\<psi\>-Y=Z>. The second is
    <math|\<mathd\>Z=\<mathd\>\<psi\>-\<mathd\>Y> and <eqref|eq:dpsi>. At
    <math|q=q<rsub|0>>, <math|P<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0> and evenness give
    <math|Y<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0=\<psi\><rsub|q<rsub|0>>>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Reading <eqref|eq:YZ-fbsde>.> The equation for <math|Y> is
  forward and explicit, once <math|Z> is known: its coefficients <math|b,H>
  are deterministic functions built from <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>>. The
  equation for <math|Z> is backward. Its terminal condition is not imposed
  but <em|proved>: if <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> matches the
  bare action as <math|q\<to\>\<infty\>>, then <math|q*Z<rsub|q>\<to\>0> at
  each fixed <math|N> (Lemma<nbsp><hlink|18.1|cqft_lecture7.tm#lem:terminal>).
  Its only source is the drift <math|b>, i.e. the gradient of the defect,
  transported by the propagator <math|exp <big|int>H>. So:

  <\padded-center>
    <em|UV stability means that the explicit action
    <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>> computes the forecast up to an error
    <math|Z> which is small uniformly in <math|N>.>
  </padded-center>

  For this we need: (i) a defect <math|e> whose gradient is integrable
  against the backward propagator, which is a deterministic renormalisation
  problem (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalisation>); (ii) global
  coercivity of <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>>, so that
  <math|H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>> is a good noise coefficient for all fields
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:large-fields>); (iii) a priori estimates for
  the coupled system (Lecture<nbsp>6). Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:strategy>
  summarises the logic of the three lectures.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<\script-output|tikz|default>
    \\documentclass[tikz]{standalone}

    \\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}

    \\usepackage[sc]{mathpazo}

    \\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,mathtools,bm}

    \\usepackage[mathscr]{eucal}

    \\usetikzlibrary{decorations.pathreplacing,arrows.meta,positioning}

    \\usepackage[dvipsnames]{xcolor}

    \;

    \\begin{document}

    \\begin{tikzpicture}[

    \ \ box/.style={draw=MidnightBlue,rounded corners=2pt,align=center,

    \ \ \ \ inner sep=4pt,font=\\small,text width=4.2cm},

    \ \ arr/.style={-{Stealth[length=2.2mm]},thick,draw=MidnightBlue!80}]

    \\node[box] (ren) at (0,0) {Renormalisation\\\\ seed $+$ 2
    corrections,\\\\ mass path (\\S13)};

    \\node[box] (clip) at (5.4,0) {Clipping: global\\\\ coercivity,
    complete\\\\ defect $b=b^{\\mathrm b}+b^{\\mathrm l}$ (\\S14)};

    \\node[box] (fbsde) at (10.8,0) {Exact FBSDE for\\\\ $(Y,Z)$ at fixed
    $N$\\\\ (Theorem~12.6)};

    \\node[box] (fwd) at (2.2,-2.4) {Forward coercivity:\\\\ large fields $J$
    by\\\\ Gaussian tails $+E$ (L6)};

    \\node[box] (bwd) at (8.6,-2.4) {Backward representation:\\\\ energy $E$
    by $b^{\\mathrm b}$,\\\\ boundary, $\\frac{C\\gamma^2}{q_0}J$ (L6)};

    \\node[box] (bd) at (0,-4.8) {Boundary $qZ_q\\to0$\\\\ at fixed $N$
    (L7)};

    \\node[box] (uv) at (5.4,-4.8) {UV stability:\\\\ $\\sup_N\\mathbb{E}\\\|
    Z_q\\\| ^2\\le Cq^{-5/2}$};

    \\node[box] (lim) at (10.8,-4.8) {Compactness, limit\\\\ FBSDE, terminal
    law,\\\\ DS identity (L7)};

    \\draw[arr] (ren)--(clip); \\draw[arr] (clip)--(fbsde);

    \\draw[arr] (fbsde)--(bwd); \\draw[arr] (clip)--(fwd);

    \\draw[arr] ([yshift=0.15cm]fwd.east) -- ([yshift=0.15cm]bwd.west);

    \\draw[arr] ([yshift=-0.15cm]bwd.west) -- ([yshift=-0.15cm]fwd.east);

    \\draw[arr] (bd)--(uv); \\draw[arr] (fwd)--(uv); \\draw[arr] (bwd)--(uv);
    \\draw[arr] (uv)--(lim);

    \\end{tikzpicture}

    \\end{document}
  </script-output|<image|<tuple|<#>|ps>||||>><label|fig:strategy>>|The
  structure of the construction. The two arrows between the forward and
  backward estimates form the closed loop of Lecture<nbsp>6: neither estimate
  closes on its own.>

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Same <math|\<Gamma\>>><label|ex:same-Gamma> Show that
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|q>> of <eqref|eq:Gamma-ex> coincides, up to a
    constant, with the <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> of
    <hlink|(8.11)|cqft_lecture4.tm#eq:moreau> for the profile
    <eqref|eq:dictionary-profile>, and that
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|q>+<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|y,K<rsub|q<rsub|0>>*y|\<rangle\>>>
    is the effective average action <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>>> of
    Theorem<nbsp><hlink|8.4|cqft_lecture4.tm#thm:wetterich>. Check that
    <eqref|eq:wetterich> agrees with <hlink|(8.12)|cqft_lecture4.tm#eq:wetterich-Gamma>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Gaussian case>Let <math|V<rsub|N><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<frac|a|2><around|\<\|\|\>|\<varphi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>,
    <math|a\<gtr\>0>. Compute <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|q>>,
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|q>>, <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|q>> and
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|q>> explicitly, and check
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:polchinski> and <eqref|eq:wetterich>.
    Compare with Exercise<nbsp><hlink|7.1|cqft_lecture4.tm#ex:quadratic>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Bayes for the whole path>Prove
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:posterior>(a) for the whole observed
    path, not only for its value at <math|q>: show that <math|P<rsub|q>> is a
    sufficient statistic.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|One site><label|ex:one-site> Take <math|N=1> (one site,
    <math|\<Delta\>=0>, <math|K<rsub|q>=q>) and
    <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<frac|\<lambda\>|4>*y<rsup|4>>.
    (a) Show that <math|Y<rsub|q>> is the real root of
    <math|q*Y+\<lambda\>*Y<rsup|3>=P<rsub|q>>, and that
    <math|H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<around|(|q+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    Explain how large fields suppress the noise of <math|Y>. (b) Compute the
    defect <math|e<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=-<frac|1|2>*<around|[|<around|(|q+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>-q<rsup|-1>|]>>
    and the drift <math|b<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=-\<gamma\>*y*<around|(|q+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-3>>.
    Show that <math|<around|\||b<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<le\>\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3><around|\||y|\|>>,
    and compare with Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:source>: on one site
    <math|a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>=q<rsup|-2>> instead of <math|q<rsup|-1/2>>,
    and no renormalisation is needed.
  </exercise>

  <section|Renormalisation: why two of them><label|sec:renormalisation>

  We now construct <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> on bounded fields.
  This section is deterministic: it uses no information on
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>>.

  <subsection|Scales and power counting>

  Two scales govern everything:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:g-r>g<rsub|q>\<assign\>\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-1/2>,<space|2em>r<rsub|q>\<assign\><around|(|q/\<gamma\>|)><rsup|1/2>,<space|2em>\<gamma\>*r<rsub|q><rsup|2>=q.
  </equation>

  <math|g<rsub|q>> is the dimensionless coupling at resolution <math|q>: in
  <math|d=3> the coupling <math|\<lambda\>> has dimension of mass, and
  <math|q<rsup|1/2>> is the mass of the regulator. It is small at all scales
  if <math|g<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=\<gamma\>*q<rsub|0><rsup|-1/2>> is small; this
  is where the large mass enters. <math|r<rsub|q>> is the field size at which
  the nonlinearity is comparable to the regulator:
  <math|D<rsup|2>*U<around|(|y|)>=\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>\<approx\>q> when
  <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<approx\>r<rsub|q>>. Compare with the size of the
  free field at resolution <math|q>: <math|<math-up|Var>X<rsub|q><around|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q>a<rsub|2>\<le\>C*q<rsup|1/2>>,
  so typical fields have size <math|q<rsup|1/4>=g<rsub|q><rsup|1/2>*r<rsub|q>\<ll\>r<rsub|q>>.

  We measure a <math|k>-tensor <math|T*<around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|k>|)>>
  (a kernel of a <math|k>th field derivative, a <em|vertex>) by its
  <em|rooted norm>

  <\equation*>
    s<around|(|T|)>=max<rsub|i> sup<rsub|x<rsub|i>>
    <big|int><around|\||T*<around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|k>|)>|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>i>\<mathd\>x<rsub|j>,
  </equation*>

  and a functional <math|f> by <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|k><around|(|f|)>=sup<rsub|y\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|q>>
  s*<around|(|D<rsup|k>*f<around|(|y|)>|)>> on the <em|field ball>

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-B\><rsub|q>\<assign\><around|{|y\<in\>H<rsub|N>:<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>2*r<rsub|q>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The natural size of the <math|k>th vertex of the loop corrections
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|q>-U-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|y,M<rsub|q>*y|\<rangle\>>>
  to the bare action is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:power-counting><math-ss|S><rsub|k>\<lesssim\>g<rsub|q>*<space|0.17em>q*<space|0.17em>r<rsub|q><rsup|2-k>.
  </equation>

  For <math|k=2> this says the Hessian of the corrections is <math|g<rsub|q>>
  times the regulator <math|q> (while <math|D<rsup|2>*U\<approx\>q> at
  <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<approx\>r<rsub|q>>); each extra field derivative
  costs <math|r<rsub|q><rsup|-1>>. For <math|k=4>,
  <math|g<rsub|q>*q*r<rsub|q><rsup|-2>=\<gamma\>*g<rsub|q>>: the quartic
  vertex of the corrections is <math|\<gamma\>> times the small coupling. The
  whole construction is a bookkeeping of <eqref|eq:power-counting>.

  <paragraph|The basic loop estimate.> Derivatives of
  <math|<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|K+D<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\>|)><rsup|-1>>
  are traces of closed chains <math|H*<space|0.17em>D<rsup|j<rsub|1>+2>*\<Gamma\>*<space|0.17em>H*\<cdots\>*D<rsup|j<rsub|p>+2>*\<Gamma\>*<space|0.17em>H>.
  A closed loop with <math|p> vertices, each carrying at least one external
  leg (vertices without external legs are resummed into <math|H>), and
  <math|p+1> propagators <math|H> (two of them adjacent, <math|H<rsup|2>>,
  once the trace is closed) is estimated by rooting one external slot, using
  two propagators in <math|L<rsup|2>> (each row has <math|L<rsup|2>> norm
  <math|\<le\><around|(|a<rsub|2>|)><rsup|1/2>>) and the others in operator
  norm (<math|\<le\>C/q>):

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:loop>s<around*|(|<text|loop>|)>\<le\>C*<space|0.17em>a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>*<space|0.17em>q<rsup|-<around|(|p-1|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|p>s<around|(|T<rsub|i>|)>
  </equation>

  <cite|FBSDE26>. The vertices of the bare action have
  <math|s*<around|(|D<rsup|j+2>*U|)>\<le\>C*q*r<rsub|q><rsup|-j>> on the ball
  (<math|D<rsup|3>*U=2*\<gamma\>*y>, <math|D<rsup|4>*U=2*\<gamma\>>). With
  <math|a<rsub|2>\<le\>C*q<rsup|-1/2>>, a loop with <math|k> external legs
  built from them is <math|\<le\>C*q<rsup|1/2>*r<rsub|q><rsup|-k>>, and after
  the integration in <math|q> that produces <math|\<Gamma\>> (which gains a
  factor <math|q>) it has size <math|q<rsup|3/2>*r<rsub|q><rsup|-k>=g<rsub|q>*q*r<rsub|q><rsup|2-k>>:
  this is the origin of <eqref|eq:power-counting>, the size of one loop.
  Replacing a bare vertex by a vertex of a correction of relative size
  <math|\<varepsilon\>> multiplies the loop by <math|\<varepsilon\>>
  (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:loop-count>). <em|Every further loop gains one
  power of <math|g<rsub|q>>.>

  <subsection|The seed: one loop>

  The first approximation is the one-loop formula of
  Exercise<nbsp><hlink|8.7|cqft_lecture4.tm#ex:1PI>(b): replace
  <math|D<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\>> by <math|D<rsup|2>*U=\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>> in the
  Wetterich equation and integrate from <math|q=\<infty\>>. With the
  quadratic part subtracted this gives the <em|seed>

  <align|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-L\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around*|[|log
  <around*|(|I+C<rsub|q><rsup|1/2>*\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>*C<rsub|q><rsup|1/2>|)>-C<rsub|q><rsup|1/2>*\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>*C<rsub|q><rsup|1/2>|]>,>>|<row|<cell|\<partial\><rsub|q>*\<cal-L\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around*|[|G<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>-C<rsub|q>|]>+<tfrac|\<gamma\>|2>a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>,<eq-number><label|eq:seed>>>>>>

  with <math|G<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<around|(|K<rsub|q>+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>.
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|q>\<le\>0>, and its constant and quadratic Taylor
  coefficients at <math|y=0> vanish. Its expansion in powers of the field,

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-L\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<ge\>2><frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|j+1>|2*j><math-up|Tr><around*|(|C<rsub|q>*\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|j>,
  </equation*>

  is the sum of one-loop diagrams with <math|2*j> external legs. The quartic
  term is the bubble <math|-<frac|\<gamma\><rsup|2>|4><big|iint>C<rsub|q>*<around|(|x,z|)><rsup|2>*y<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>*y<around|(|z|)><rsup|2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>x<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>z>,
  with <math|s*<around|(|D<rsup|4>*\<cal-L\><rsub|q><around|(|0|)>|)>=6*\<gamma\><rsup|2>*a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>\<le\>C*g<rsub|q>*q*r<rsub|q><rsup|-2>>,
  as predicted by <eqref|eq:power-counting>.

  <subsection|Corrections and the mass>

  The seed solves the Wetterich equation only with <math|D<rsup|2>*U> inside
  the trace. Its defect is of relative size <math|g<rsub|q>> compared with
  the one-loop flow <math|q<rsup|1/2>*r<rsub|q><rsup|-k>> (two loops). To
  improve it, let <math|\<Pi\>*f<around|(|y|)>=f<around|(|y|)>-f<around|(|0|)>-<frac|1|2>*D<rsup|2>*f<around|(|0|)><around|[|y,y|]>>
  remove the constant and quadratic Taylor coefficients (the <em|low jets>)
  of an even functional, let <math|M<rsub|q>=\<mu\><rsub|q>*I+\<Theta\><rsub|q>>
  be a quadratic operator path (a running mass <math|\<mu\><rsub|q>> and an
  even, translation-invariant, centred kernel <math|\<Theta\><rsub|q>>,
  <math|\<Theta\><rsub|q>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>=0>), and set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:trace-map><with|math-font|cal*|T><rsub|q>*<around|[|M,h|]><around|(|y|)>\<assign\><tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around*|[|<around*|(|K<rsub|q>+M<rsub|q>+D<rsup|2>*<around|(|U+h|)><around|(|y|)>|)><rsup|-1>-<around*|(|K<rsub|q>+M<rsub|q>|)><rsup|-1>|]>.
  </equation>

  The approximate action on the field ball is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:Gamma-o>\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|o>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=U<around|(|y|)>+<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|y,M<rsub|q>*y|\<rangle\>>+h<rsup|<around|[|2|]>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>,
  </equation>

  with the nonlinear part built by two <em|integrated> Wetterich steps,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:iteration>h<rsup|<around|[|0|]>>=\<cal-L\>,<space|2em>h<rsup|<around|[|j+1|]>><rsub|q>=\<cal-L\><rsub|q>-<big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>>\<Pi\>*<around*|(|<with|math-font|cal*|T><rsub|s>*<around|[|M,h<rsup|<around|[|j|]>>|]>-\<partial\><rsub|s>*\<cal-L\><rsub|s>|)>\<mathd\>s,<space|2em>j=0,1,
  </equation>

  so that <math|\<partial\><rsub|q>*h<rsup|<around|[|j+1|]>>=\<Pi\>*<space|0.17em><with|math-font|cal*|T><rsub|q>*<around|[|M,h<rsup|<around|[|j|]>>|]>>:
  each step inserts the previous approximation into the trace. The integrals
  run from <math|\<infty\>>: the nonlinear part is fixed by the requirement
  that it vanishes at infinite resolution, where <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|q>>
  must become the bare action (Theorem<nbsp><hlink|8.4|cqft_lecture4.tm#thm:wetterich>).

  <paragraph|The quadratic part.> The low jets are not iterated: they are
  handled exactly by the path <math|M>. The quadratic part of the flow at
  <math|y=0> is, by evenness, <math|<frac|1|2>*D<rsup|2><math-up|Tr>H<around|(|0|)>=-<frac|1|2><math-up|Ctr><rsub|R<rsub|0><rsup|2>>D<rsup|4>*\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|o>><around|(|0|)>>,
  where <math|R<rsub|0,q>=<around|(|K<rsub|q>+M<rsub|q>|)><rsup|-1>> and
  <math|<math-up|Ctr><rsub|R>T> contracts two slots of <math|T> with the
  kernel <math|R>. Requiring that <math|M> solves this part of the flow
  exactly gives the <em|mass equation>

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:mass>\<partial\><rsub|q>*M<rsub|q>=B<rsub|q><around|[|M|]>\<assign\>-\<gamma\><math-up|Tr><around*|(|R<rsub|0,q><rsup|2>|)>*I-<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Ctr><rsub|R<rsub|0,q><rsup|2>>D<rsup|4>*h<rsup|<around|[|2|]>><rsub|q><around|(|0|)>,
  </equation>

  (the source <math|B<rsub|q><around|[|M|]>> is not the sampling Brownian
  motion <math|B>), with the <em|mixed boundary conditions>

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\><rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0,<space|2em>\<Theta\><rsub|q>\<to\>0*<text|as
    >q\<to\>\<infty\>
  </equation*>

  (the latter in first spatial moment). The running mass is pinned at the
  physical scale <math|q<rsub|0>>, while the nonlocal kernel is pinned at
  infinity. The <em|bare mass> is then

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:bare-mass>\<mu\><rsub|N>=lim<rsub|q\<to\>\<infty\>>
    \<mu\><rsub|q>=<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|\<infty\>>\<beta\><rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q,<space|2em>B<rsub|q>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>=\<beta\><rsub|q>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>,
  </equation>

  and it is this number that enters the Gibbs measure <eqref|eq:nuN>. Note
  the order: the counterterm is an output of the deterministic flow, not an
  input.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The coefficient construction<nbsp><cite|FBSDE26>><label|prop:coeff>
    For <math|q<rsub|0>> large enough (depending on <math|\<gamma\>>, not on
    <math|N>), the system <eqref|eq:iteration>\U<eqref|eq:mass> has a unique
    solution in a ball, with

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:coeff><around|\||\<mu\><rsub|q>|\|>\<le\>C*\<gamma\>*<sqrt|q>,<space|2em>v<rsub|1><around|(|\<Theta\><rsub|q>|)>\<le\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|2>*q<rsup|-1/2>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsub|q>*M<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|row>>\<le\>C*g<rsub|q>,
    </equation>

    where <math|v<rsub|1><around|(|\<Theta\>|)>=<big|int>d<around|(|0,z|)>*<around|\||\<Theta\>*<around|(|0,z|)>|\|>\<mathd\>z>.
    The seed obeys <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|k,q><around|(|\<cal-L\>|)>\<le\>C<rsub|k>*g<rsub|q>*q*r<rsub|q><rsup|2-k>>,
    <math|4\<le\>k\<le\>10>, and the corrections obey

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:increments>\<cal-N\><rsub|k,q>*<around*|(|h<rsup|<around|[|j|]>>-h<rsup|<around|[|j-1|]>>|)>\<le\>C<rsub|k>*<space|0.17em>g<rsub|q><rsup|j+1>*<space|0.17em>q*<space|0.17em>r<rsub|q><rsup|2-k>,<space|2em>4\<le\>k\<le\>10-2*j,<space|1em>j=1,2,
    </equation>

    where <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|k,q><around|(|f|)>=<math-ss|S><rsub|k><around|(|f|)>+<sqrt|q>*<space|0.17em>v<rsub|1>*<around|(|D<rsup|k>*f<around|(|0|)>|)>>,
    and for a <math|k>-tensor

    <\equation*>
      v<rsub|1><around|(|T|)>=max<rsub|i,a\<less\>b> sup<rsub|x<rsub|i>>
      <big|int>d<around|(|x<rsub|a>,x<rsub|b>|)><around|\||T|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>i>\<mathd\>x<rsub|j>.
    </equation*>

    The complete defect of <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|o>>> on the ball is
    <math|e<rsup|<math-up|o>><rsub|q>=\<Pi\>*<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|T><rsub|q>*<around|[|M,h<rsup|<around|[|1|]>>|]>-<with|math-font|cal*|T><rsub|q>*<around|[|M,h<rsup|<around|[|2|]>>|]>|)>>,
    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:defect-bound><math-ss|S><rsub|4><around*|(|e<rsup|<math-up|o>><rsub|q>|)>\<le\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|5>*q<rsup|-3>.
    </equation>

    At fixed <math|N> the bare mass exists,
    <math|<around|\||\<mu\><rsub|N>|\|>\<lesssim\>\<gamma\><math-up|Tr>C\<lesssim\>\<gamma\>*N>,
    and <math|<around|\||\<mu\><rsub|N>-\<mu\><rsub|q>|\|>\<le\>C*\<gamma\>*N<rsup|3>/q>,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Theta\><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|row>>\<le\>C*N*\<gamma\><rsup|2>*q<rsup|-1/2>\<to\>0>
    as <math|q\<to\>\<infty\>>.
  </proposition>

  <\render-proof|Idea of the proof>
    (a) <em|Trace Lipschitz estimate.> If two corrections <math|h,<wide|h|^>>
    obey the individual bounds <eqref|eq:power-counting> up to order
    <math|k+2> and their difference obeys them with <math|g<rsub|q>> replaced
    by <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>>, then
    <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|k>*<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|T>*<around|[|M,h|]>-<with|math-font|cal*|T>*<around|[|M,<wide|h|^>|]>|)>\<le\>C*\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>*q<rsup|1/2>*r<rsub|q><rsup|-k>>.
    Expand <math|D<rsup|k>> of the trace into loops; in each, telescope the
    difference onto one marked vertex or propagator and apply
    <eqref|eq:loop>. (b) <em|Integration.> Each step gains one <math|g>; the
    integrand of <eqref|eq:iteration> is <math|O<around|(|\<gamma\><rsup|1+k/2>*s<rsup|-k/2>|)>>
    in the <math|k>th derivative, and that of the increment
    <math|h<rsup|<around|[|j+1|]>>-h<rsup|<around|[|j|]>>> is
    <math|O<around|(|\<gamma\><rsup|1+k/2>*s<rsup|-k/2>*g<rsub|s><rsup|j>|)>>,
    integrable at <math|s=\<infty\>> for <math|k\<ge\>4>. The low jets have
    been removed, so the lower derivatives are controlled by Taylor's formula
    from the fourth. (c) <em|Mass path.> Given <math|M> in the ball, the
    right side of <eqref|eq:mass> is computed from the corrections, and the
    map <math|M\<mapsto\><around|(|\<mu\>,\<Theta\>|)>> is a contraction for
    large <math|q<rsub|0>>: differences gain a factor
    <math|g<rsub|q<rsub|0>>> (for <math|\<mu\>> after integrating from
    <math|q<rsub|0>>, using <math|sup<rsub|x> log
    x/<sqrt|x>\<less\>\<infty\>>). (d) <em|Defect.> The mass equation
    supplies exactly the quadratic jet of the flow, and
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|q>*h<rsup|<around|[|2|]>>=\<Pi\>*<with|math-font|cal*|T>*<around|[|M,h<rsup|<around|[|1|]>>|]>>,
    so the defect is the trace difference between the last two
    approximations, of order <math|g<rsup|3>>: by (a) with
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>=g<rsub|q><rsup|3>>,
    <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|e<rsup|<math-up|o>>|)>\<le\>C*g<rsub|q><rsup|3>*q<rsup|1/2>*r<rsub|q><rsup|-4>=C*\<gamma\><rsup|5>*q<rsup|-3>>.
  </render-proof>

  Two technical points are hidden in this sketch. First, the trace estimate
  (a) needs more derivatives than it controls: the <math|k>th derivative of
  the trace involves vertices of order <math|k+2>. Ten derivatives of the
  seed leave eight after one step and six after two; this is why the number
  of steps must be finite and fixed in advance (an infinite iteration loses
  all derivatives). Second, the quadratic kernel <math|\<Theta\><rsub|q>>
  must be measured in a first spatial moment <math|v<rsub|1>>, not in a
  second one: the estimate <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<delta\><rsub|z><rsup|2>*C<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|row>>\<lesssim\>d<around|(|0,z|)><rsup|2>>,
  which a second moment would require, fails by a logarithm, and one uses
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<delta\><rsub|z><rsup|2>*C<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|row>>\<lesssim\>q<rsup|-1/2>*d<around|(|0,z|)>>
  instead <cite|FBSDE26>. This is also why <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|k>> contains
  a spatial moment at <math|y=0>.

  <subsection|Two mass renormalisations>

  The bare mass <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>> diverges as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>.
  Its two divergent parts are the two divergent self-energy diagrams of
  Section<nbsp><hlink|10.2|cqft_lecture4.tm#sec:pert>.

  <\itemize>
    <item><em|The tadpole.> The first term of <eqref|eq:mass> integrates to
    <math|-\<gamma\>*<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|\<infty\>><math-up|Tr>R<rsub|0,q><rsup|2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q\<approx\>-\<gamma\><math-up|Tr>C=-3*\<lambda\><math-up|Tr>C\<sim\>-N>.
    This is Wick ordering: <math|<frac|\<lambda\>|4>*\<varphi\><rsup|4>-<frac|3|2>*\<lambda\>*c*\<varphi\><rsup|2>>
    agrees, up to a constant, with <math|<frac|\<lambda\>|4>*H<rsub|4>*<around|(|\<varphi\>;c|)>>,
    with <math|c=C*<around|(|x,x|)>> (<hlink|(10.5)|cqft_lecture4.tm#eq:first-order>).

    <item><em|The sunset.> The second term contracts the bubble contained in
    <math|D<rsup|4>*h<rsup|<around|[|2|]>><around|(|0|)>> (the quartic
    coefficient of the seed) with <math|R<rsub|0><rsup|2>>, and integrating
    in <math|q> produces the two-loop self-energy, which diverges like
    <math|\<lambda\><rsup|2>*<big|int><rsub|<around|\||z|\|>\<gtr\>1/N><around|\||z|\|><rsup|-3>\<mathd\>z\<sim\>\<lambda\><rsup|2>*log
    N>. This is the sunset counterterm <math|\<gamma\><rsub|T>\<sim\>log T>
    of <hlink|(10.7)|cqft_lecture4.tm#eq:gammaT>: with
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|<math-up|L3>>=\<lambda\>/4> and
    <math|\<gamma\><rsub|T>*<big|int>H<rsub|2>=\<gamma\><rsub|T>*<big|int>\<varphi\><rsup|2>+>const,
    the sunset part of <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>> is
    <math|2*\<gamma\><rsub|T>=6*\<lambda\><rsup|2>*<big|int>C<rsup|3>\<approx\><frac|3*\<lambda\><rsup|2>|8*\<pi\><rsup|2>>*log
    N>, positive. (The double-tadpole logarithms in the two terms of
    <eqref|eq:mass>, the bubble with a tadpole insertion and the mass
    insertion in <math|<math-up|Tr>R<rsub|0,q><rsup|2>>, cancel each other.)
  </itemize>

  Power counting (Exercise<nbsp><hlink|10.3|cqft_lecture4.tm#ex:power-counting>)
  says that no other counterterm is needed. In the flow this is the statement
  that the integrals <eqref|eq:iteration> of all vertices with
  <math|k\<ge\>4> legs converge at <math|q=\<infty\>>: only the quadratic jet
  must be fixed by a boundary condition.

  There is a subtlety: the running mass <math|\<mu\><rsub|q>> at fixed
  <math|q> has a limit as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>, but
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>=\<mu\><rsub|\<infty\>>> does not. One never
  interchanges these two limits. Also, the <em|whole> running kernel
  <math|\<Theta\><rsub|q>> is kept: replacing the sunset by its local part
  would leave a nonlocal remainder of the wrong size.

  <subsection|Two correction steps>

  Why two corrections, and not one or three? The answer is in the backward
  equation of <eqref|eq:YZ-fbsde>. Freeze the noise and the large fields, and
  estimate the size of <math|Z> produced by a defect on the ball. The
  propagator of <math|\<mathd\>Z=-H*Z<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q+\<ldots\>> from
  <math|q> to <math|s> is about <math|s/q> (since <math|H\<approx\>C<rsub|q>>
  has norm <math|q<rsup|-1>>), so

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:Z-heuristic>Z<rsub|q>\<approx\><big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|s|q>*<space|0.17em>b<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s,<space|2em><around|\||b<rsub|s>|\|>\<lesssim\><around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em><math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|e<rsub|s>|)>*<space|0.17em>r<rsub|s><rsup|3>\<lesssim\>s<rsup|-1><math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|e<rsub|s>|)>*<space|0.17em>r<rsub|s><rsup|3>,
  </equation>

  where we used that the low jets of <math|e> vanish, so on the ball
  <math|<around|\||D*e|\|>\<le\><math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|e|)><around|\||y|\|><rsup|3>/6>.
  After <math|n> correction steps the defect is the trace difference of the
  last two approximations, of relative size <math|g<rsup|n+1>>: by the trace
  estimate,

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|e<rsub|s>|)>\<lesssim\>g<rsub|s><rsup|n+1>*s<rsup|1/2>*r<rsub|s><rsup|-4>=\<gamma\><rsup|n+3>*s<rsup|-<around|(|n+4|)>/2>,<space|2em><around|\||b<rsub|s>|\|>\<lesssim\>\<gamma\><rsup|n+3/2>*s<rsup|-<around|(|n+3|)>/2>,
  </equation*>

  and therefore

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||Z<rsub|q>|\|>\<lesssim\><frac|\<gamma\><rsup|n+3/2>|q>*<big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>>s<rsup|-<around|(|n+1|)>/2>\<mathd\>s.
  </equation*>

  <\padded-center>
    <with|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|4|4|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|steps
    <math|n>>|<cell|<math|<math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|e<rsub|s>|)>>>|<cell|<math|<around|\||b<rsub|s>|\|>>>|<cell|<math|<big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|s|q><around|\||b<rsub|s>|\|>\<mathd\>s>>>|<row|<cell|<math|0>
    (seed only)>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|3>*s<rsup|-2>>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|3/2>*s<rsup|-3/2>>>|<cell|<math|\<infty\>>
    (power)>>|<row|<cell|<math|1>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|4>*s<rsup|-5/2>>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|5/2>*s<rsup|-2>>>|<cell|<math|\<infty\>>
    (logarithm)>>|<row|<cell|<math|2>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|5>*s<rsup|-3>>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|7/2>*s<rsup|-5/2>>>|<cell|<math|2*\<gamma\><rsup|7/2>*q<rsup|-3/2>>>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  With one step the bounded-field source produces a logarithmically divergent
  error; two steps are the minimum, and they suffice:
  <math|Z<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q>=O<around|(|q<rsup|-3/2>|)>>, and the
  backward energy <math|\<gamma\><rsup|-1>*<big|int>q<rsup|1/2><around|\||Z<rsup|<math-up|b>>|\|><rsup|2>\<mathd\>q>
  is finite (Lecture<nbsp>6). The general (fractional) theory gives the depth
  criterion <math|<around|(|n+2|)>*\<varkappa\>\<gtr\><around|(|3+\<varkappa\>|)>/2>,
  with subcriticality <math|\<varkappa\>=<frac|1|2>> for
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>: <math|<around|(|2+2|)>\<cdot\><frac|1|2>=2\<gtr\><frac|7|4>>,
  while <math|n=1> gives <math|<frac|3|2>\<less\><frac|7|4>>. This is a
  sufficient condition, which loses <math|\<varkappa\>/2>; for
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> the heuristic above shows that
  <math|n\<gtr\>1> suffices, with <math|n=1> borderline.

  The two senses of \Ptwo renormalisations\Q are related. The mass
  counterterm must contain the sunset because the quartic vertex of the
  corrections, already of the seed, contains the bubble, and contracting it
  produces the sunset; and the second step is needed because the defect of
  the first one, the three-loop remainder, is borderline in the backward
  equation, exactly as the sunset is borderline in the mass.

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Counting loops><label|ex:loop-count> (a) Use <eqref|eq:loop> to
    show that a loop with <math|p> vertices
    <math|D<rsup|j<rsub|i>+2>*<around|(|U+h|)>>, <math|j<rsub|i>\<ge\>1>,
    each with <math|s\<le\>C*q*r<rsub|q><rsup|-j<rsub|i>>>, and
    <math|k=<big|sum>j<rsub|i>> external legs has rooted norm
    <math|\<le\>C*a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>*<space|0.17em>q*<space|0.17em>r<rsub|q><rsup|-k>\<le\>C*q<rsup|1/2>*r<rsub|q><rsup|-k>>.
    (b) Show that <math|q\<cdot\>q<rsup|1/2>*r<rsub|q><rsup|-k>=g<rsub|q>*q*r<rsub|q><rsup|2-k>>.
    (c) If one vertex is replaced by a vertex of size
    <math|\<varepsilon\>*<space|0.17em>q*r<rsub|q><rsup|-j>>, show that the
    loop gains the factor <math|\<varepsilon\>>. Deduce the sizes
    <math|g<rsup|j+1>> of the increments in <eqref|eq:increments>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|The bubble>Compute <math|D<rsup|4>*\<cal-L\><rsub|q><around|(|0|)>>.
    Show that its rooted norm is <math|6*\<gamma\><rsup|2>*a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>>
    and its first spatial moment is <math|O<around|(|\<gamma\><rsup|2>*q<rsup|-1>|)>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Small Hessians are not enough>On one site take
    <math|q=\<gamma\>=1>, <math|M=0>, fixed small
    <math|\<alpha\>,\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|<wide|h|^><rsub|n><around|(|y|)>=\<alpha\>*n<rsup|-2>*<around|(|cos
    <around|(|n*y|)>-1+n<rsup|2>*y<rsup|2>/2|)>>,
    <math|h<rsub|n>=<wide|h|^><rsub|n>+<frac|\<delta\>|12>*y<rsup|4>>. Show
    that both have small Hessians on <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<less\>2>, that
    <math|h<rsub|n>-<wide|h|^><rsub|n>> has bounded derivatives, but
    <math|D<rsup|4>*<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|T><around|[|h<rsub|n>|]>-<with|math-font|cal*|T><around|[|<wide|h|^><rsub|n>|]>|)><around|(|0|)>=12*<around|(|2*\<delta\>+\<alpha\>*\<delta\>*n<rsup|2>+\<delta\><rsup|2>|)>>
    is unbounded in <math|n>. Conclude that the trace estimate needs the
    individual higher derivative bounds.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Why two steps>Verify the table above. With one step, show that the
    bounded-field part of <math|Z> is of size <math|q<rsup|-1>*log
    <around|(|T/q|)>> on <math|<around|[|q,T|]>>, for an upper scale cutoff
    <math|T>.
  </exercise>

  <section|The flow at large fields><label|sec:large-fields>

  <subsection|The large-field problem>

  All the estimates of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:coeff> hold on the
  field ball <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>2*r<rsub|q>>,
  and only there. But the forecast <math|Y<rsub|q>> is a random field on
  <math|N<rsup|3>> sites, and nothing prevents it from being larger than
  <math|r<rsub|q>> somewhere. The free field has typical size
  <math|q<rsup|1/4>\<ll\>r<rsub|q>>, but it exceeds <math|r<rsub|q>> with
  small, nonzero probability, and nothing a priori controls the interacting
  forecast. We need an action <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> defined
  and <em|uniformly convex for all fields>, which agrees with
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|o>><rsub|q>> on the ball.

  <paragraph|A scalar model.> The difficulty is already visible in the
  one-site seed, <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<frac|1|2>*<around|[|log
  <around|(|1+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>/q|)>-\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>/q|]>>. Its power
  series <math|<big|sum><rsub|j\<ge\>2><frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|j+1>|2*j>*<around|(|\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>/q|)><rsup|j>>
  has radius of convergence exactly <math|r<rsub|q>>: the singularities are
  at <math|y=\<pm\>\<mathi\>r<rsub|q>>. Truncating it at any order produces a
  polynomial whose top coefficient has sign <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|j+1>>:
  for even <math|j\<ge\>4> the truncation is unbounded below and destroys the
  convexity of <math|U+\<cal-L\>> at <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<gg\>r<rsub|q>>.
  The exact function is harmless: its logarithmic part grows only
  logarithmically, and <math|<around|\||\<cal-L\><rsub|q><rprime|''><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<le\><frac|9|8>*\<gamma\>/q\<le\>2*\<gamma\>/q>
  for all <math|y>. In field theory we do not have the exact function, only
  bounds on the ball, obtained through an expansion of the trace in vertices
  (the corrections are not polynomials, but their estimates are expansions in
  the field). This is the <em|large-field problem>: perturbative control
  holds for <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<lesssim\>r<rsub|q>>, and outside one
  needs a non-perturbative replacement that preserves the structure the
  estimates use.

  The structure to preserve is coercivity, and it is carried by the bare
  quartic term. In the scalar model the coordinate equation
  <math|q*y+\<lambda\>*y<rsup|3>=p> has noise coefficient

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<frac|1|q+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>>\<le\><frac|1|q>\<wedge\><frac|1|\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>>:
  </equation*>

  large fields are <em|more> elliptic and less noisy. A good large-field
  extension keeps <math|U> untouched and freezes only the correction.

  <subsection|Clipping>

  Let <math|\<tau\>:\<bbb-R\>\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> be smooth and odd,
  <math|\<tau\><around|(|u|)>=u> on <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>>, constant
  outside <math|<around|[|-2,2|]>>, with <math|0\<le\>\<tau\><rprime|'>\<le\>1>,
  and set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:clipping>\<kappa\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)><around|(|x|)>\<assign\>r<rsub|q>*<space|0.17em>\<tau\>*<around*|(|y<around|(|x|)>/r<rsub|q>|)>,<space|2em><wide|h|~><rsub|q>\<assign\>h<rsup|<around|[|2|]>><rsub|q>\<circ\>\<kappa\><rsub|q>,<space|2em><wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>\<assign\>U+<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|y,M<rsub|q>*y|\<rangle\>>+<wide|h|~><rsub|q>.
  </equation>

  The clip acts pointwise in space and maps every field into the ball. The
  quartic and quadratic parts are not clipped. By the chain rule, with
  factors <math|<around|\||\<kappa\><rsub|q><rsup|<around|(|b|)>>|\|>\<le\>C*r<rsub|q><rsup|1-b>>,
  the clipped correction obeys the same bounds <eqref|eq:power-counting>
  <em|globally>:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:global-h>s*<around|(|D<rsup|j>*<wide|h|~><rsub|q>|)>\<le\>C*g<rsub|q>*q*r<rsub|q><rsup|2-j>*<around|(|j=2,3,4|)>,<space|2em>s*<around|(|D<rsup|2>*\<partial\><rsub|q>*<wide|h|~><rsub|q>|)>\<le\>C*g<rsub|q>,
  </equation>

  for all <math|y\<in\>H<rsub|N>>; the scale derivative includes the motion
  of the clip, <math|\<partial\><rsub|q>*\<kappa\><rsub|q>=O<around|(|r<rsub|q>/q|)>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Global coercivity><label|lem:resolvent> For <math|q<rsub|0>>
    large, for all <math|y\<in\>H<rsub|N>> and <math|q\<ge\>q<rsub|0>>,

    <eqnarray*|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|c*<space|0.17em><around|(|K<rsub|q>+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)>\<le\>H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>\<le\>C*<space|0.17em><around|(|K<rsub|q>+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)>,>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\>p>\<le\><frac|1+C*<around|(|g<rsub|q>+g<rsub|q><rsup|2>|)>|q>*<space|1em><around|(|1\<le\>p\<le\>\<infty\>|)>,<space|2em><math-up|diag>H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)><rsup|2>\<le\>C*a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>.>|<eq-number><label|eq:resolvent>>>>>
  </lemma>

  <\render-proof|Idea>
    <math|H<rsup|-1>=K<rsub|q>+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>+M<rsub|q>+D<rsup|2>*<wide|h|~><rsub|q>>,
    and <math|M<rsub|q>+D<rsup|2>*<wide|h|~><rsub|q>> is small relative to
    <math|K<rsub|q>>: <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|2>*<wide|h|~><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>>\<le\>C*g<rsub|q>*q>,
    and <math|M<rsub|q>> is small after multiplication by <math|C<rsub|q>>
    (the centred kernel is controlled by its first moment,
    Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:receive>). Write
    <math|H=<around|(|I+G*<around|(|M+D<rsup|2>*<wide|h|~>|)>|)><rsup|-1>*G>
    with <math|G=<around|(|K<rsub|q>+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>> and use
    the Neumann series and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:free>.
  </render-proof>

  So <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> is a globally convex action, and
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:YZ> applies.

  <subsection|The complete defect>

  On the set <math|<around|{|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<le\>r<rsub|q>|}>>
  the clip is the identity and the defect is <math|e<rsup|<math-up|o>>>, of
  size <eqref|eq:defect-bound>. Outside it, the clipped action does not solve
  the Wetterich equation even approximately in relative terms. Split the
  drift into its bounded-field and large-field parts,

  <\equation*>
    b<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>\<assign\>-H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>D*\<kappa\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>D*e<rsup|<math-up|o>><rsub|q>*<around|(|\<kappa\><rsub|q>*y|)>,<space|2em>b<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|q>\<assign\>b<rsub|q>-b<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q>,
  </equation*>

  and let <math|\<chi\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|\||y|\|>\<gtr\>r<rsub|q>>>
  (pointwise) and <math|t<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=y*<space|0.17em>\<chi\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>>,
  the field restricted to the large-field set.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Complete source bounds<nbsp><cite|FBSDE26>><label|prop:source>
    Uniformly in <math|y\<in\>H<rsub|N>>, <math|N> and
    <math|p\<in\><around|[|1,\<infty\>|]>>,

    <align|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>|<cell|\<le\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|7/2>*q<rsup|-5/2>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>|<cell|\<le\>C*\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3/2><around*|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|q>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|p>+r<rsub|q><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>*y<rsup|2>*\<chi\><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>>|<cell|\<le\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|4>*q<rsup|-5><around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6>.<eq-number><label|eq:source>>>>>>

    Moreover <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<le\>C*\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3/2><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>.
  </proposition>

  The first bound is <eqref|eq:Z-heuristic> with <math|n=2>: on the ball
  <math|<around|\||D*e<rsup|<math-up|o>>|\|>\<le\><math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|e<rsup|<math-up|o>>|)>*<around|(|2*r|)><rsup|3>/6>,
  and <math|q<rsup|-1>*\<gamma\><rsup|5>*q<rsup|-3>*r<rsub|q><rsup|3>=\<gamma\><rsup|7/2>*q<rsup|-5/2>>.
  The large-field part is as large as the bound
  <math|\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3/2><around|\||y|\|>=<around|(|g<rsub|q>/q|)><around|\||y|\|>>
  on the whole drift (no smallness beyond the factor <math|g<rsub|q>> already
  present in <math|b>), but it is <em|supported on the large-field set> (up
  to nonlocal tails controlled by operator bounds). The last bound follows
  from the second since on <math|<around|{|<around|\||y|\|>\<gtr\>r|}>>,
  <math|<around|\||t|\|>+r<rsup|-1>*y<rsup|2>\<le\>2*r<rsup|-2><around|\||t|\|><rsup|3>>,
  and <math|<around|(|\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3/2>*r<rsup|-2>|)><rsup|2>=\<gamma\><rsup|4>*q<rsup|-5>>.

  <paragraph|Where the large-field error comes from.> Compute the gradient of
  the defect of the clipped action and compare it with
  <math|D*\<kappa\>*<space|0.17em>D*e<rsup|<math-up|o>>*<around|(|\<kappa\>*y|)>>.
  With <math|z=\<kappa\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>>,
  <math|J=D*\<kappa\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>> (multiplication by
  <math|\<tau\><rprime|'>*<around|(|y/r|)>>),
  <math|h=h<rsup|<around|[|2|]>><rsub|q>>,
  <math|M<rprime|'>=\<partial\><rsub|q>*M<rsub|q>>, and the unclipped
  response <math|H<rsup|<math-up|o>>=<around|(|K+M+\<gamma\>*z<rsup|2>+D<rsup|2>*h<around|(|z|)>|)><rsup|-1>>,
  the difference has four groups (seven terms):

  <\enumerate-roman>
    <item>the mass term <math|M<rprime|'>*y-J*M<rprime|'>*z>: the quadratic
    part is not clipped, but its flow was computed at <math|z>;

    <item>the motion of the clip, <math|J*D<rsup|2>*h<around|(|z|)>*\<partial\><rsub|q>*\<kappa\>+<around|(|D*\<partial\><rsub|q>*\<kappa\>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*D*h<around|(|z|)>>;

    <item><math|\<gamma\>*<around|(|y-J*z|)><math-up|diag>H<rsup|2>> and
    <math|\<gamma\>*J*z<math-up|diag><around|(|H<rsup|2>-<around|(|H<rsup|<math-up|o>>|)><rsup|2>|)>>:
    the quartic vertex sees <math|y>, the correction sees <math|z>, and the
    two responses differ by <math|\<gamma\>*<around|(|y<rsup|2>-z<rsup|2>|)>>
    and by the chain-rule terms of the clipped Hessian, all supported on
    <math|<around|{|<around|\||y|\|>\<gtr\>r<rsub|q>|}>>;

    <item>the contraction of <math|D<rsup|3>*<around|(|h\<circ\>\<kappa\>|)>-J*D<rsup|3>*h<around|(|z|)>>
    (the chain rule produces terms with <math|\<kappa\><rprime|''>>,
    <math|\<kappa\><rprime|'''>>, supported on large fields) and of
    <math|D<rsup|3>*h> with <math|H<rsup|2>-<around|(|H<rsup|<math-up|o>>|)><rsup|2>>.
  </enumerate-roman>

  Each group vanishes if <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<le\>r<rsub|q>>,
  and each is bounded by <math|g<rsub|q>> times <math|<around|\||t|\|>> or
  <math|r<rsup|-1>*y<rsup|2>*\<chi\>>, in <math|L<rsup|p>>, by the same loop
  estimates (<cite|FBSDE26>; Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:clip-scalar> for the
  one-site version). There is no cancellation to exploit: the clipping error
  is genuinely of the size <math|<around|(|g<rsub|q>/q|)><around|\||y|\|>> of
  the whole drift on the large-field set. What saves us is that this set is
  rarely visited, and its occupation is controlled by the coercivity of the
  actual dynamics. Lecture<nbsp>6 proves exactly this, with the weighted
  <em|large-field action>

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|\<infty\>>q<rsup|-3/2>*<space|0.17em>\<gamma\><around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q,
  </equation*>

  (the <math|t>-part of the large-field action of Lecture<nbsp>6), whose
  weight is matched to the last bound in <eqref|eq:source>:
  <math|q<rsup|7/2>\<cdot\>\<gamma\><rsup|4>*q<rsup|-5>=\<gamma\><rsup|3>\<cdot\><around|(|\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3/2>|)>>,
  so <math|<big|int>q<rsup|7/2><around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<mathd\>q\<le\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|3>*<big|int>q<rsup|-3/2>*\<gamma\><around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6>\<mathd\>q>,
  where <math|q<rsup|7/2>> is the weight with which
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>>
  enters the backward representation (Lecture<nbsp>6).

  <paragraph|The coordinate correction.> Finally, write the approximate
  coordinate equation as

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coordinate>K<rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|q>=K<rsub|q>*<around*|(|Y<rsub|q>+\<varrho\><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>|)>+<tfrac|\<gamma\>|3>Y<rsub|q><rsup|3>,<space|2em>\<varrho\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>\<assign\>C<rsub|q>*<around*|(|M<rsub|q>*y+D*<wide|h|~><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|)>,
  </equation>

  using <math|D*U<around|(|y|)>=\<lambda\>*y<rsup|3>=<frac|\<gamma\>|3>*y<rsup|3>>.
  The correction is a small contraction, <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<varrho\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\>p>\<le\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>\<assign\>C*<around|(|g<rsub|q>+g<rsub|q><rsup|2>|)>>,
  for all <math|y>. So, up to a small Lipschitz perturbation, <em|the
  forecast is the solution of the cubic elliptic equation
  <math|K<rsub|q>*<around|(|Y+\<varrho\>|)>+<frac|\<gamma\>|3>*Y<rsup|3>=K<rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|q>>
  driven by the field at resolution <math|q>>. This is the equation whose
  coercivity Lecture<nbsp>6 exploits.

  <subsection|Summary>

  At the end of this lecture we have, at every lattice cutoff:

  <\itemize>
    <item>a counterterm <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>>, a mass path <math|M<rsub|q>>
    and a globally convex action <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>=U+<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|y,M<rsub|q>*y|\<rangle\>>+h<rsup|<around|[|2|]>><rsub|q>\<circ\>\<kappa\><rsub|q>>,
    all deterministic and constructed without any information on
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>>;

    <item>the exact FBSDE <eqref|eq:YZ-fbsde> for the approximate forecast
    <math|Y> and the error <math|Z>, with noise coefficients bounded as in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:resolvent>;

    <item>a source <math|b=b<rsup|<math-up|b>>+b<rsup|<math-up|l>>>, whose
    bounded-field part is integrable in the backward equation thanks to the
    two correction steps, and whose large-field part is controlled by sixth
    powers of <math|Y> on the large-field set.
  </itemize>

  Lecture<nbsp>6 turns these deterministic bounds into estimates on
  <math|<around|(|Y,Z|)>> uniform in <math|N>.

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Receiving a centred kernel><label|ex:receive> Let <math|\<Theta\>>
    be a translation-invariant, even kernel with
    <math|\<Theta\>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>=0>. With <math|T<rsub|z>> the
    translation by <math|z>, <math|<around|(|T<rsub|z>*f|)><around|(|x|)>=f*<around|(|x+z|)>>,
    and using <math|C<rsub|q>*\<Theta\>=<frac|1|2>*<big|int>\<Theta\><around|(|z|)>*<around|(|T<rsub|z>+T<rsub|-z>-2|)>*C<rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>z>,
    show that <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|q>*\<Theta\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|row>>\<le\>C*<space|0.17em>q<rsup|-1/2>*v<rsub|1><around|(|\<Theta\>|)>>
    if <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|T<rsub|z>+T<rsub|-z>-2|)>*C<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|row>>\<le\>C*q<rsup|-1/2>*d<around|(|0,z|)>>.
    Why is the absolute row mass of <math|\<Theta\>> not the right quantity?
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|The scalar clipping error><label|ex:clip-scalar> On one site, let
    <math|h<around|(|y|)>=-\<beta\>*<frac|\<gamma\><rsup|2>|4*q<rsup|2>>*y<rsup|4>>
    (a quartic correction, <math|\<beta\>> a fixed number) and
    <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>=<frac|\<lambda\>|4>*y<rsup|4>+h<around|(|\<kappa\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|)>>.
    (a) Show that <math|K<rsub|q>+<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><rprime|''>\<ge\>q/2>
    for all <math|y> if <math|\<beta\>*\<gamma\>\<le\>c*q<rsup|2>>. (b) Show
    that <math|b<rsub|q>=b<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q>> for
    <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<le\>r<rsub|q>>. For
    <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<gtr\>2*r<rsub|q>> show that
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|q>> is locally constant,
    <math|b<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=0>, and
    <math|b<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=-\<gamma\>*y*<around|(|q+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-3>>,
    the drift of the pure quartic. (c) Conclude that on the large-field set
    the error is the whole drift of the trace term: it is comparable, for
    <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<approx\>2*r<rsub|q>>, to the one-site bound
    <math|\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3><around|\||y|\|>> on the whole drift (the
    analogue of <math|<around|(|g<rsub|q>/q|)><around|\||y|\|>>, with
    <math|a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>=q<rsup|-2>> in place of
    <math|q<rsup|-1/2>>), but it vanishes on the ball. Compare with
    <eqref|eq:source>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Large fields are less noisy>On one site with
    <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>=<frac|\<lambda\>|4>*y<rsup|4>>, show that
    the quadratic variation of <math|Y> is
    <math|<big|int>H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)><rsup|2>\<mathd\>q> and
    that <math|H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)><rsup|2>\<le\><around|(|\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-2>>.
    Simulate the observation process for <math|\<nu\>=\<mathe\><rsup|-\<lambda\>*\<varphi\><rsup|4>/4>\<mu\><rsup|1/q<rsub|0>>>
    and plot <math|P<rsub|q>>, <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>=P<rsub|q>/q>,
    <math|Y<rsub|q>> and <math|\<psi\><rsub|q>>.
  </exercise>

